  "worked_solution": "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{-5}{1\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{6} \\\\  & ≈-0.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
